WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.590
A good commissioner can tell, from the first pages of a staff report, what decision is actually being asked.

2
00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:07.091
Use the same pass every time.

3
00:00:07.091 --> 00:00:13.458
It comes from the way the Institute for Local Government teaches project review, tightened for a California housing agenda.

4
00:00:13.458 --> 00:00:14.742
First, name the action.

5
00:00:14.742 --> 00:00:17.255
Is this ministerial, and therefore not yours?

6
00:00:17.255 --> 00:00:24.569
Is it a conditional use permit, a variance, a map, a design review, or a legislative recommendation to change the plan or the zone?

7
00:00:24.569 --> 00:00:27.361
The findings you must make are different for each.

8
00:00:27.361 --> 00:00:37.858
In Torrance a conditional use permit requires eleven findings, including compatibility with the neighborhood, a suitable site, adequate access and services, and no harmful noise or hazard.

9
00:00:37.858 --> 00:00:43.553
Do not borrow those findings for a variance, and do not borrow variance findings for a plan amendment.

10
00:00:43.553 --> 00:00:44.949
Second, test consistency.

11
00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:48.801
What is the general plan designation, and what does the zoning allow?

12
00:00:48.801 --> 00:00:50.197
If they conflict, say so.

13
00:00:50.197 --> 00:00:58.684
For housing, consistency is judged against objective standards: rules an applicant can measure before filing, with no room for a personal judgment call.

14
00:00:58.684 --> 00:01:01.085
A height limit of thirty feet is objective.

15
00:01:01.085 --> 00:01:05.105
A rule that upper floors must "step back to ease the transition" is not.

16
00:01:05.105 --> 00:01:11.079
A court of appeal said so in California Renters Legal Advocacy and Education Fund versus City of San Mateo.

17
00:01:11.079 --> 00:01:18.617
If a housing project meets the objective rules, the Housing Accountability Act sharply limits your power to deny it or cut its density.

18
00:01:18.617 --> 00:01:20.747
Third, walk the site without romance.

19
00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:30.763
What is next door, and does the use clash with it in a way the code actually regulates: truck noise against bedrooms, a driveway through a sidewalk, grading on a steep slope?

20
00:01:30.763 --> 00:01:36.462
Can people reach it on foot, on a bike, in a car, and with a delivery truck, or do the paths cross?

21
00:01:36.462 --> 00:01:44.579
Is there a listed historic building, a floodplain, a very high fire zone, or a habitat constraint that a specific written standard addresses?

22
00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:48.091
Neighborhood character, standing alone, is not that standard.

23
00:01:48.091 --> 00:01:50.336
Fourth, read the numbers the code uses.

24
00:01:50.336 --> 00:01:56.035
Height, setbacks, lot coverage, floor area, parking, private open space, window area on the street.

25
00:01:56.035 --> 00:02:03.000
Write down each one the project misses, and then ask whether a density-bonus waiver or concession has already excused it.

26
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.684
A missed standard that state law waives is not a denial finding.

27
00:02:06.684 --> 00:02:09.102
Fifth, read the environmental cover sheet.

28
00:02:09.102 --> 00:02:13.937
Exempt, negative declaration, mitigated negative declaration, or full impact report.

29
00:02:13.937 --> 00:02:19.061
If it is exempt, the fight will be about whether every condition of the exemption is met.

30
00:02:19.061 --> 00:02:24.990
If it is not, you will need the baseline, the thresholds, and the mitigation, which is its own episode.

31
00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:28.392
Then look at the economics only as far as the law allows you to.

32
00:02:28.392 --> 00:02:31.740
A condition that is legal can still make a project unbuildable.

33
00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:36.524
Ask what the condition costs per home and whether a cheaper design meets the same purpose.

34
00:02:36.524 --> 00:02:45.825
Torrance keeps a small share of the property tax and a much larger share of sales tax, so replacing a store with apartments is a fiscal question as well as a housing question.

35
00:02:45.825 --> 00:02:49.970
Ask staff what the site produces in taxes now and what it would produce after.

36
00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:54.807
Close the pass by drafting, in your notes, the sentence you would say if you voted tonight.

37
00:02:54.807 --> 00:03:02.567
If you cannot tie that sentence to a finding and a page of the record, you are not ready, and the right move is a question to staff, not a speech.
